  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to use a high-resolution representation (n=640) combined with a stochastic optimization method to reduce the maximum correlation between h and its shifted versions. This follows from the observation that higher resolution allows capturing finer features, which may lead to a better solution. The algorithm employs global and local perturbations to explore the search space effectively.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent the step function h as a vector of length n=640 samples over [0,2], with the integral constraint sum_i h_i = 320 (since the dx factor is 2/640). The goal is to minimize C5, defined as the maximum correlation between h and 1-h using mode=\"full\". The correlation is scaled by dx=2/640.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Given the parent solution at n=160, interpolate it to n=640 by linear interpolation over [0,2].\n2. For each iteration:\n   a. With probability 0.1 (global move), perturb by adding a small Gaussian noise proportional to the standard deviation (0.1.\n   b. Otherwise (local move), pick three random indices and adjust them such that the sum of changes is zero (one index increased, two decreased proportionally).\n   c. Project the resulting candidate to satisfy the box [0,1] and integral constraints.\n   d. Evaluate the candidate using np.correlate and find the max correlation.\n3. Update the best solution if a lower C5 is found.\n4. Run until the budget is exhausted or a significant improvement is found.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\nThe default strategy uses interpolation, 0.1 probability of global moves, and a 0.1 magnitude for Gaussian noise. We focus on the interpolated parent solution (n=640). Adaptations include:\n- Global move probability (0.1): tunable from 0.01 to 0.2.\n- Noise magnitude (0.1): tunable from 0.05 to 0.2.\n- Resolution (640): can try n=320 or n=1280.\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\nThe main cost is the objective computation (O(n^2)), which is 400,000 operations for n=640, roughly 0.006 seconds per evaluation. The total evaluation budget should be around 216,666, keeping the CPU utilization high. The algorithm will return the best candidate encountered by the budget deadline.\n\nValidation and return contract:\nCandidates are checked for integral-1.0 and [0,1.0]. C5 is computed exactly. On termination, the candidate is re-evaluated to ensure accuracy.",
